      <h1>Lead-Acid Plate &amp; Grid Design</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Technology Reference · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> A lead-acid cell is built from <strong>positive plates</strong> (lead-dioxide
        active material) and <strong>negative plates</strong> (spongy lead), each held on a <strong>lead-alloy grid</strong>.
        The grid conducts current and supports the active material; plate <em>thickness and count</em> are the design
        lever that trades cranking power against cycle life — which is why two batteries with the same Ah can have
        different CCA.
      </div>

      <h2>The two jobs of the grid</h2>
      <p>The grid is not the active material — it is the skeleton. It does two things:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Conducts current</strong> from the active material to the terminal.</li>
        <li><strong>Holds the active material</strong> in place mechanically.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The grid is a lead alloy, not pure lead, because alloying adds strength and changes behavior:</p>

      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Grid alloy</th><th>Character</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Lead–calcium (Pb–Ca)</td><td>Low water loss, low self-discharge, "maintenance-free"</td><td>SLI / start-stop / AGM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lead–antimony (Pb–Sb)</td><td>Stronger grid, better deep cycling, higher water loss</td><td>Deep-cycle / traction</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lead–tin (Pb–Sn, additive)</td><td>Improves castability and corrosion resistance</td><td>Used with the above</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The plate-count trade-off (why same Ah ≠ same CCA)</h2>
      <p>For a fixed case size, the designer can choose <strong>more, thinner plates</strong> or <strong>fewer, thicker plates</strong>:</p>

      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Design</th><th>Surface area</th><th>CCA (cranking)</th><th>Cycle life</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>More, thinner plates</td><td>Higher</td><td><strong>Higher</strong></td><td>Lower (fragile, less active material reserve)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fewer, thicker plates</td><td>Lower</td><td>Lower</td><td><strong>Higher</strong> (durable, deep-cycle friendly)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>CCA is largely a <strong>surface-area</strong> game: more plate surface means more simultaneous reaction,
      so more instantaneous current. Capacity (Ah) is a <strong>mass-of-active-material</strong> game. A starting
      battery optimizes surface area; a deep-cycle battery optimizes material mass and durability. That is the
      concrete mechanism behind "a battery can be strong in one and modest in the other."</p>
      <h2>The alloy numbers</h2>
      <p>
        The grid alloy quietly sets the battery's character: <strong>calcium alloys</strong> cut water loss and
        self-discharge (the maintenance-free and AGM choice), while <strong>antimony alloys</strong> add
        deep-cycle endurance at the cost of more water consumption — the flooded deep-cycle tradition. The alloy
        is why two batteries with identical plates can need different maintenance (see
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">starting vs deep-cycle</a>).
      </p>


      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My take:</strong> When a spec sheet gives Ah but hides the plate design, it is hiding the one
        fact that explains the CCA number — plate geometry is the difference, not marketing.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> Ah and CCA are not convertible precisely because they live in different parts
        of the cell — mass versus surface area. Once you see plates as the dial between them, most "mysterious" rating
        gaps disappear.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My view as an editor, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

